.TH KIYOME 1 "2026-10-27" "kiyome" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
kiyome \- wipe a disk with five verified patterns, naming any bad bit line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B kiyome
.br
.B kiyome
.RI [ options ] " device" | mount_point
.br
.B kiyome doctor
.br
.B kiyome version
.br
.B kiyome help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B kiyome
writes per-chunk unique AES-CTR data, then 0xAA, 0x55, 0xFF and 0x00 over the
whole disk, and reads every pattern back. A wrong byte is reported with the bit
lines involved, which tells a stuck data line apart from worn media. Every
chunk is timed against limits measured on the disk itself, so a drive that
stalls is stopped and ejected before it damages the media.
.PP
A disk is named by its device, such as
.IR /dev/disk4 ,
or by any volume mounted from it, such as
.IR /Volumes/USB ,
which stands for the whole disk.
.PP
Before anything is written, kiyome prints what it is about to erase and asks
for the device name to be typed back.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B doctor
List the tools kiyome needs and whether each is installed. Exits non-zero only
when a required tool is missing.
.TP
.B version
Print the version and the platform.
.TP
.B help
Print the usage page.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-passes " N"
Repeat the five patterns
.I N
times. Default 1.
.TP
.B \-\-create
Leave a fresh FAT or exFAT filesystem behind.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-internal
Allow an internal disk. The disk holding the running system is always refused.
.PP
Every option that takes a value also takes the form
.IR \-\-name = value ,
and
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PASSES
Same as
.BR \-\-passes .
.TP
.B CREATE
Set to
.B y
for the same effect as
.BR \-\-create .
.TP
.B ALLOW_INTERNAL
Set to
.B y
for the same effect as
.BR \-\-allow\-internal .
.PP
An option on the command line wins over the environment.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when every pattern read back as written, 1 on any failure or refusal.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kakikomi (1),
.BR dd (1)
